1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,098
Twelve orders, five sales reps.

2
00:00:02,098 --> 00:00:03,172
Who sold the most?

3
00:00:03,172 --> 00:00:08,956
Instead of adding it up by hand, you type one formula, and Excel builds the whole report for you.

4
00:00:08,956 --> 00:00:10,328
Every rep, every total.

5
00:00:10,328 --> 00:00:12,952
And it updates itself when the data changes.

6
00:00:13,651 --> 00:00:20,961
In the next ten minutes, you'll learn seven Excel formulas that replace hours of copying, filtering and adding things up by hand.

7
00:00:20,961 --> 00:00:23,907
One small table, step by step, no experience needed.

8
00:00:23,907 --> 00:00:31,047
And around the middle, I'll show you the error that confuses almost everyone the first time, and how to fix it in two seconds.

9
00:00:31,747 --> 00:00:33,039
Here's the list.

10
00:00:33,039 --> 00:00:34,976
XLOOKUP finds any value.

11
00:00:34,976 --> 00:00:37,882
FILTER pulls out every matching row.

12
00:00:37,882 --> 00:00:39,981
UNIQUE removes duplicates.

13
00:00:39,981 --> 00:00:42,161
SORT puts a table in order.

14
00:00:42,161 --> 00:00:45,310
SUMIFS adds up numbers with conditions.

15
00:00:45,310 --> 00:00:47,570
IFS labels rows using rules.

16
00:00:47,570 --> 00:00:51,284
And TEXTSPLIT breaks text into separate cells.

17
00:00:51,284 --> 00:00:52,495
One quick note.

18
00:00:52,495 --> 00:00:57,258
These need Excel for Microsoft 365, or Excel 2021 or newer.

19
00:00:57,258 --> 00:01:00,891
TEXTSPLIT needs Microsoft 365, or Excel 2024.

20
00:01:01,591 --> 00:01:03,160
Here's our practice table.

21
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:09,680
The order number is in column A, the sales rep in B, the region in C, the product in D, and the amount in E.

22
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:12,639
Twelve orders, from row two down to row thirteen.

23
00:01:12,639 --> 00:01:15,416
Type it in, or use any table you already have.

24
00:01:15,416 --> 00:01:17,770
The formulas work exactly the same way.

25
00:01:18,470 --> 00:01:19,652
Formula one, XLOOKUP.

26
00:01:19,652 --> 00:01:24,550
It finds a value in one column, and brings back the matching value from another column.

27
00:01:24,550 --> 00:01:25,788
It needs three things.

28
00:01:25,788 --> 00:01:29,335
What you're looking for, where to look, and what to bring back.

29
00:01:29,335 --> 00:01:32,037
The fourth one is optional, but I always use it.

30
00:01:32,037 --> 00:01:34,457
It's the text to show when nothing matches.

31
00:01:35,157 --> 00:01:37,962
Let's find the amount for order ten oh seven.

32
00:01:37,962 --> 00:01:39,708
Type the order number in G2.

33
00:01:39,708 --> 00:01:50,616
Then click H2 and type equals XLOOKUP, open bracket, G2, comma, A2 colon A13, that's the order column, comma, E2 colon E13, the amount column, comma, and Not found, in quotes.

34
00:01:50,616 --> 00:01:51,364
Press Enter.

35
00:01:51,364 --> 00:01:52,174
Twelve fifty.

36
00:01:52,174 --> 00:01:53,982
That's exactly the right row.

37
00:01:54,682 --> 00:01:59,145
Now change G2 to an order that doesn't exist, like ten ninety nine.

38
00:01:59,145 --> 00:02:02,609
Instead of an ugly error, you get a clean Not found.

39
00:02:02,609 --> 00:02:06,339
Without that fourth argument, Excel would show hash N A.

40
00:02:07,039 --> 00:02:08,261
Here's a pro move.

41
00:02:08,261 --> 00:02:11,317
The return array can be more than one column.

42
00:02:11,317 --> 00:02:16,410
Change E2 colon E13 to B2 colon E13, and XLOOKUP brings back the whole row.

43
00:02:16,410 --> 00:02:21,639
Rep, region, product and amount, spilled across four cells, from one formula.

44
00:02:22,339 --> 00:02:23,582
Formula two, FILTER.

45
00:02:23,582 --> 00:02:27,993
Give it a table and a condition, and it returns every row that matches.

46
00:02:27,993 --> 00:02:29,422
Not just the first one.

47
00:02:29,422 --> 00:02:30,167
All of them.

48
00:02:30,167 --> 00:02:34,081
The optional third argument is what to show if nothing matches.

49
00:02:34,781 --> 00:02:42,499
Click G2 and type equals FILTER, A2 colon E13, that's the whole table, comma, then C2 colon C13 equals West, in quotes.

50
00:02:42,499 --> 00:02:43,277
Press Enter.

51
00:02:43,277 --> 00:02:48,790
Five rows, every order from the West region, in the same order as the original table.

52
00:02:48,790 --> 00:02:53,265
And if you change a region in the table, this list updates by itself.

53
00:02:53,965 --> 00:02:55,068
Need two conditions?

54
00:02:55,068 --> 00:02:58,157
Put each one in brackets, and multiply them with a star.

55
00:02:58,157 --> 00:03:00,970
Region is West, and the amount is over one hundred.

56
00:03:00,970 --> 00:03:06,818
Each condition gives TRUE or FALSE for every row, and multiplying keeps only the rows where both are TRUE.

57
00:03:06,818 --> 00:03:08,307
So the star works like AND.

58
00:03:08,307 --> 00:03:09,190
Two orders left.

59
00:03:09,190 --> 00:03:10,955
For OR, use a plus sign instead.

60
00:03:11,655 --> 00:03:12,728
One thing to know.

61
00:03:12,728 --> 00:03:17,917
If nothing matches, and you skipped the third argument, FILTER shows a hash CALC error.

62
00:03:17,917 --> 00:03:22,927
Add a message, like No orders, as the third argument, and you get that text instead.

63
00:03:23,627 --> 00:03:26,078
Now, the error that confuses almost everyone.

64
00:03:26,078 --> 00:03:30,215
You type the exact same formula, and instead of results, you get hash SPILL.

65
00:03:30,215 --> 00:03:30,433
Why?

66
00:03:30,433 --> 00:03:31,686
Look at the dashed box.

67
00:03:31,686 --> 00:03:33,646
That's where the results want to go.

68
00:03:33,646 --> 00:03:35,823
But cell H4 already has something in it.

69
00:03:35,823 --> 00:03:38,546
Excel refuses to overwrite your data, so it stops.

70
00:03:38,546 --> 00:03:39,961
The fix takes two seconds.

71
00:03:39,961 --> 00:03:43,881
Delete or move whatever is in the way, and the results spill right back.

72
00:03:44,581 --> 00:03:45,960
Formula three, UNIQUE.

73
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:48,405
It gives you a list without duplicates.

74
00:03:48,405 --> 00:03:51,351
Click G2, and type equals UNIQUE, B2 colon B13.

75
00:03:51,351 --> 00:03:53,983
Twelve rows, but only five different reps.

76
00:03:53,983 --> 00:03:57,744
Ana, Ben, Cara, Dev and Eli, in the order they first appear.

77
00:03:57,744 --> 00:04:02,382
No more copying a column and clicking Remove Duplicates every single week.

78
00:04:03,082 --> 00:04:04,145
Formula four, SORT.

79
00:04:04,145 --> 00:04:09,234
Type equals SORT, then the table, then the number of the column to sort by, then the order.

80
00:04:09,234 --> 00:04:12,702
Five means the fifth column of the table, which is the amount.

81
00:04:12,702 --> 00:04:14,604
And minus one means largest first.

82
00:04:14,604 --> 00:04:16,561
Plus one would mean smallest first.

83
00:04:16,561 --> 00:04:19,190
Press Enter, and the biggest orders are on top.

84
00:04:19,190 --> 00:04:21,651
Your original table stays exactly as it was.

85
00:04:22,351 --> 00:04:25,199
And you can put one formula inside another.

86
00:04:25,199 --> 00:04:30,364
SORT of UNIQUE of the region column gives you an alphabetical list of regions.

87
00:04:30,364 --> 00:04:32,020
East, North, South, West.

88
00:04:32,020 --> 00:04:34,669
Perfect as the first column of a report.

89
00:04:35,369 --> 00:04:36,582
Formula five, SUMIFS.

90
00:04:36,582 --> 00:04:40,339
It adds up numbers, but only the ones that match your conditions.

91
00:04:40,339 --> 00:04:41,957
Careful with the order here.

92
00:04:41,957 --> 00:04:44,210
The column you want to add comes first.

93
00:04:44,210 --> 00:04:47,215
Then a column to check, and the value it must match.

94
00:04:47,215 --> 00:04:50,567
And you can keep adding more pairs of range and condition.

95
00:04:51,267 --> 00:04:52,791
Total sales for Ana.

96
00:04:52,791 --> 00:04:58,581
Equals SUMIFS, E2 colon E13, comma, B2 colon B13, comma, and Ana, in quotes.

97
00:04:58,581 --> 00:05:02,847
Twelve hundred, plus eighty, plus three hundred and ten.

98
00:05:02,847 --> 00:05:03,990
Fifteen ninety.

99
00:05:04,690 --> 00:05:05,713
Two conditions?

100
00:05:05,713 --> 00:05:07,214
Just add another pair.

101
00:05:07,214 --> 00:05:09,805
Region is West, and product is Laptop.

102
00:05:09,805 --> 00:05:13,830
Only one order matches both, so the answer is eleven fifty.

103
00:05:14,530 --> 00:05:15,373
Now, the bonus.

104
00:05:15,373 --> 00:05:17,568
The report from the start of the video.

105
00:05:17,568 --> 00:05:20,043
In G2, type equals UNIQUE of the rep column.

106
00:05:20,043 --> 00:05:26,625
Then in H2, type equals SUMIFS, the amount column, the rep column, and for the condition, G2 followed by a hash sign.

107
00:05:26,625 --> 00:05:29,945
G2 hash means the whole spilled list, however long it gets.

108
00:05:29,945 --> 00:05:32,195
Press Enter, and every rep gets a total.

109
00:05:32,195 --> 00:05:36,021
Add a new rep to the table tomorrow, and the report grows by itself.

110
00:05:36,721 --> 00:05:37,688
Formula six, IFS.

111
00:05:37,688 --> 00:05:42,410
It checks conditions in order, and returns the result of the first one that's TRUE.

112
00:05:42,410 --> 00:05:43,776
Let's label every order.

113
00:05:43,776 --> 00:05:45,483
One thousand or more is Large.

114
00:05:45,483 --> 00:05:47,189
One hundred or more is Medium.

115
00:05:47,189 --> 00:05:50,148
And the last pair, TRUE comma Small, is a catch-all.

116
00:05:50,148 --> 00:05:53,391
TRUE is always true, so anything left over becomes Small.

117
00:05:54,091 --> 00:05:56,026
Type it in F2, and press Enter.

118
00:05:56,026 --> 00:06:03,143
Then double click the small square in the corner of the cell, called the fill handle, to copy it all the way down.

119
00:06:03,143 --> 00:06:04,454
Large, Small, Medium.

120
00:06:04,454 --> 00:06:06,452
Every order labeled in a second.

121
00:06:07,152 --> 00:06:08,427
A very common mistake.

122
00:06:08,427 --> 00:06:16,488
If you check one hundred or more first, an order of twelve hundred stops right there, because twelve hundred is also more than one hundred.

123
00:06:16,488 --> 00:06:19,735
It gets labeled Medium, and Large never shows up at all.

124
00:06:19,735 --> 00:06:23,736
IFS stops at the first TRUE, so always test the biggest number first.

125
00:06:24,436 --> 00:06:25,972
Formula seven, TEXTSPLIT.

126
00:06:25,972 --> 00:06:30,580
You have full names in one column, and you need first and last names apart.

127
00:06:30,580 --> 00:06:34,450
In B2, type equals TEXTSPLIT, A2, comma, and a space in quotes.

128
00:06:34,450 --> 00:06:37,767
The space is the delimiter, the character to split on.

129
00:06:37,767 --> 00:06:40,041
Ana goes into B, Lopez spills into C.

130
00:06:40,041 --> 00:06:42,621
Copy it down, and the whole list is split.

131
00:06:43,321 --> 00:06:45,684
Want the pieces down a column instead?

132
00:06:45,684 --> 00:06:51,095
Leave the second argument empty, with two commas in a row, and put the delimiter third.

133
00:06:51,095 --> 00:06:52,650
That's the row delimiter.

134
00:06:52,650 --> 00:06:55,511
One cell with four products becomes four rows.

135
00:06:56,211 --> 00:06:56,764
Heads up.

136
00:06:56,764 --> 00:07:01,071
TEXTSPLIT always returns text, even when the pieces look like numbers.

137
00:07:01,071 --> 00:07:07,100
If you split something like ten comma twenty, and want to add the results, wrap it in VALUE first.

138
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:09,049
Quick troubleshooting.

139
00:07:09,049 --> 00:07:12,058
Hash NAME means Excel doesn't recognize the function.

140
00:07:12,058 --> 00:07:15,806
Either there's a typo, or your version of Excel is too old for it.

141
00:07:15,806 --> 00:07:18,702
Hash SPILL means something is blocking the results.

142
00:07:18,702 --> 00:07:22,393
Hash N A from XLOOKUP means no match, so add the fourth argument.

143
00:07:22,393 --> 00:07:26,368
And hash CALC from FILTER means nothing matched, so add the third one.

144
00:07:27,068 --> 00:07:27,552
Pro tip.

145
00:07:27,552 --> 00:07:32,688
Click anywhere inside your data, and press Control T, to turn it into an Excel Table.

146
00:07:32,688 --> 00:07:39,818
Then, when you add new rows at the bottom, any formula that uses the table's column names includes them automatically.

147
00:07:39,818 --> 00:07:43,141
No more changing thirteen to fourteen in every formula.

148
00:07:43,841 --> 00:07:44,626
Quick recap.

149
00:07:44,626 --> 00:07:46,065
XLOOKUP finds a value.

150
00:07:46,065 --> 00:07:48,289
FILTER returns every matching row.

151
00:07:48,289 --> 00:07:49,990
UNIQUE removes duplicates.

152
00:07:49,990 --> 00:07:51,691
SORT orders by any column.

153
00:07:51,691 --> 00:07:53,523
SUMIFS adds with conditions.

154
00:07:53,523 --> 00:07:55,878
IFS labels rows, biggest test first.

155
00:07:55,878 --> 00:07:57,971
And TEXTSPLIT breaks text apart.

156
00:07:57,971 --> 00:08:00,980
Seven formulas, and hours of manual work gone.

157
00:08:01,680 --> 00:08:11,165
Next on Shortcut Lab, we switch to AI, with five prompt techniques that actually work, so you get better answers from any chatbot on the first try.

158
00:08:11,165 --> 00:08:13,230
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.
